<remote-review>[{"bug_id": "bug_003", "name": "[quality] Layout comment misdescribes how Next.js finds the nonce", "severity": "nit", "file_path": "app/layout.tsx", "start_line": 28, "end_line": 32, "pr_comment": "The comment on `app/layout.tsx:28-32` misstates the mechanism: Next.js reads its bootstrap-script nonce from the `x-nonce` **request** header that `proxy.ts` forwards via `NextResponse.next({ request: { headers: requestHeaders } })`, not from the response's CSP header (which is only what the browser enforces). Reword to point at `x-nonce` so a future maintainer doesn't misread `proxy.ts:42`'s `requestHeaders.set(\"x-nonce\", nonce)` as dead code and delete it, silently breaking nonce propagation.", "reasoning": "**What the comment says vs. how it actually works.** The layout comment claims: \"Next.js automatically tags its own bootstrap `\u003cscript\u003e` elements with the nonce from the response's CSP header, so we don't need to read x-nonce here ourselves.\" The first half is factually inverted. Next.js's SSR pipeline learns the nonce for its own bootstrap chunks by reading the `x-nonce` **request** header that middleware/proxy attaches (via `NextResponse.next({ request: { headers } })`). The response `Content-Security-Policy` header is what the browser enforces to block un-nonced scripts \u2014 Next.js never re-parses it to discover its own nonce. This is documented in Next.js's official CSP-with-nonces guide.\\n\\n**Why the wrong wording is dangerous here.** `proxy.ts:41-42` does two related things: builds a CSP with `'nonce-${nonce}'` on the response, and calls `requestHeaders.set(\"x-nonce\", nonce)` then forwards those headers via `NextResponse.next({ request: { headers: requestHeaders } })`. The layout doesn't call `headers().get('x-nonce')` (it only awaits `headers()` to opt out of static rendering), so the request-header forwarding looks superfluous if you believe the layout comment. It isn't \u2014 it's exactly the wire that lets Next.js nonce its own bootstrap scripts.\\n\\n**Concrete failure mode (step-by-step).** 1) A future maintainer touches `proxy.ts` and, referencing the layout comment, concludes the request-header plumbing is dead code. 2) They delete `requestHeaders.set(\"x-nonce\", nonce)` and simplify to `NextResponse.next()`. 3) Locally, `npm run dev` still renders because dev-mode CSP behavior is looser. 4) In production, Next.js's server renders bootstrap `\u003cscript\u003e` tags without a `nonce=` attribute (there's no `x-nonce` request header to pick up). 5) The browser evaluates the strict CSP (`script-src 'self' 'nonce-\u2026' 'strict-dynamic'`) \u2014 `'strict-dynamic'` disables the `'self'` allowlist, so any un-nonced script (including Next's own chunks) is blocked. 6) The app fails to hydrate; users see a blank page or a shell with no interactivity, and console fills with CSP violations.\\n\\n**Why existing code doesn't prevent it.** Nothing in TypeScript, ESLint, or the test suite catches a stale comment. The linkage between the layout comment and the proxy's request-header forwarding is only readable to humans, so the wrong description directly determines what a reader concludes.\\n\\n**Impact and fix.** Impact is low today (the code works) but latent \u2014 the comment is a maintenance trap in the same PR that introduces both files, so the risk lands the moment either file is touched. Fix: tighten the second sentence to reference `x-nonce`, e.g. \"Next.js reads the nonce from the `x-nonce` request header that proxy.ts forwards to server components, and automatically tags its own bootstrap `\u003cscript\u003e` elements with it, so we don't need to read `x-nonce` here ourselves.\" Zero runtime change, preserves the correct intent.", "corroborated_by": null, "source_bug_ids": ["bug_003"], "created_at": 1786744404.1167886, "_file_path": "/tmp/bughunter_work/reports/bug_003_report.json"}, {"bug_id": "bug_006", "name": "connect-src 'self' blocks PNG export fetch(dataUrl)", "severity": "normal", "file_path": "proxy.ts", "start_line": 26, "end_line": 26, "pr_comment": "The new `connect-src 'self'` in the CSP breaks the graph PNG export: `app/lib/utils/exportGraph.ts` calls `fetch(dataUrl)` on a `data:` URL produced by html-to-image, and per CSP L3 `connect-src 'self'` does not match `data:` (opaque origin), so both `downloadGraphAsPng` (Download-as-PNG button in GraphPanel) and `graphToPngBlob` (graph image inside the ZIP export in `exportAll.ts`) fail silently \u2014 the try/catch at GraphPanel.tsx:105-107 and exportAll.ts:67-69 only `console.error`, so users see the click do nothing. Fix by either adding `data:` to `connect-src` in `proxy.ts`, or (preferred, no CSP loosening) rewriting the data:\u2192Blob conversion in exportGraph.ts using `atob` + `Uint8Array` + `new Blob([bytes], { type: 'image/png' })`.", "reasoning": "### What breaks\n\nThis PR adds a strict CSP with `connect-src 'self'` to every page navigation (`proxy.ts:26`). Per the CSP Level 3 spec, `connect-src` governs the URLs a page may reach via `fetch()` / XHR / WebSocket / EventSource, and the `'self'` source expression matches only the document's own scheme + host + port. A `data:` URL has an **opaque origin** \u2014 it does not match `'self'`. To permit `fetch()` on data URIs, `data:` must be listed explicitly in `connect-src`. The `img-src 'self' data:` entry in the new CSP does **not** help here \u2014 `img-src` covers `\u003cimg\u003e` and CSS `background-image`, not `fetch()`.\n\n### The code path\n\n`app/lib/utils/exportGraph.ts` uses html-to-image's `toPng` (which resolves to a base64 `data:image/png;base64,...` URL) and then converts it to a Blob by fetching it:\n\n```ts\n// exportGraph.ts:20-26\nconst dataUrl = await toPng(viewportElement, { pixelRatio: 2, backgroundColor: EXPORT_BG });\nconst res = await fetch(dataUrl);      // \u003c-- blocked by connect-src 'self'\nconst blob = await res.blob();\ntriggerDownload(blob, filename);\n```\n\nThe same pattern appears at `exportGraph.ts:33-38` in `graphToPngBlob`, which is used by `exportAll.ts:64` to embed the graph image in the ZIP export.\n\nBoth callers only `console.error` on failure:\n- `GraphPanel.tsx:100-109` \u2014 the Download-as-PNG button: `catch (err) { console.error(\"[graph export]\", err); }`\n- `exportAll.ts:67-69` \u2014 the ZIP builder: `catch (err) { console.warn(\"[export] Could not capture graph image:\", err); }`\n\nSo the failure mode is silent: users click Download-as-PNG and nothing happens, or they get a ZIP without `proof-graph.png` inside.\n\n### Step-by-step proof\n\n1. Ship this PR. All page responses now carry `Content-Security-Policy: ...; connect-src 'self'; ...`.\n2. User opens the decomposition/graph panel and clicks the \"Export as PNG\" button (`GraphPanel.tsx:98-110`).\n3. `handleExportGraph` dynamic-imports `exportGraph.ts` and calls `downloadGraphAsPng(viewport)`.\n4. `toPng(...)` resolves with `dataUrl = \"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSU...\"`.\n5. `await fetch(dataUrl)` executes. The browser checks `connect-src` for a match; `data:` does not match `'self'`, so the fetch is refused with `TypeError: Failed to fetch` and the console shows `Refused to connect to 'data:image/png;base64,...' because it violates the following Content Security Policy directive: \"connect-src 'self'\"`.\n6. The `try/catch` in GraphPanel logs the error and returns; `setExporting(false)` fires but no file is downloaded, no toast is shown.\n7. Same failure occurs for the ZIP export path: `exportAll.ts:64` calls `graphToPngBlob`, which hits the same blocked `fetch(dataUrl)`; the outer `try/catch` swallows it and the ZIP is produced without the graph image.\n\n### Why this wasn't caught in review\n\nThe CSP's `img-src 'self' data: blob:` entry looks like it covers data URIs, and it does \u2014 for `\u003cimg\u003e`. But the graph export doesn't use an `\u003cimg\u003e`; it does `fetch(dataUrl).then(r =\u003e r.blob())` as an idiomatic way to build a Blob from a data URL. That request is governed by `connect-src`, not `img-src`. This is a well-known strict-CSP gotcha in canvas-export flows and is enforced in both Chromium and Firefox.\n\n### Fix\n\nTwo options:\n\n1. **Preferred \u2014 no CSP loosening.** Rewrite `exportGraph.ts` to convert the data URL to a Blob directly, no fetch:\n   ```ts\n   const [, base64] = dataUrl.split(\",\");\n   const bytes = Uint8Array.from(atob(base64), c =\u003e c.charCodeAt(0));\n   const blob = new Blob([bytes], { type: \"image/png\" });\n   ```\n2. Add `data:` to `connect-src` in `proxy.ts:26`. Simpler diff but slightly loosens CSP \u2014 every future `fetch()` call in the app will also be allowed to hit data URIs, which was presumably not the intent of a \"strict\" CSP.\n\nOption 1 also removes an unnecessary network round-trip on the export path.", "corroborated_by": null, "source_bug_ids": ["bug_006"], "created_at": 1786744423.9020445, "_file_path": "/tmp/bughunter_work/reports/bug_006_report.json"}, {"bug_id": "bug_002", "name": "Strict CSP has no dev-mode carve-out \u2014 breaks HMR/error overlay", "severity": "normal", "file_path": "proxy.ts", "start_line": 19, "end_line": 32, "pr_comment": "**Strict CSP is applied unconditionally** \u2014 no `NODE_ENV` gate, no `'unsafe-eval'` in dev. Next.js dev mode (Turbopack/webpack HMR + React Refresh) evaluates modules via `eval`/`new Function`, and `'strict-dynamic' does NOT grant eval permission per the CSP spec. Every developer running the first command documented in `CLAUDE.md` (`npm run dev`) will hit console CSP violations that break HMR reconnection and the error overlay. Fix: gate the strict CSP on `process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'`, or add `'unsafe-eval'` to `script-src` only in development.", "reasoning": "## What breaks\n\n`proxy.ts` builds the same CSP string in every environment (lines 19-32). `script-src` contains `'self'`, a per-request nonce, and `'strict-dynamic'` \u2014 but no `'unsafe-eval'`. That is the correct posture for production, but Next.js dev mode does not work under it.\n\n## Why `strict-dynamic` does not save this\n\nPer the CSP3 spec, `'strict-dynamic'` propagates trust to scripts *loaded by* trusted scripts (via `createElement(\"script\")` and similar). It explicitly does **not** grant `eval`/`new Function`/`setTimeout(string, ...)` permission \u2014 those still require `'unsafe-eval'`. Next.js dev mode relies on eval-based module evaluation for React Refresh / Fast Refresh: the HMR runtime wraps hot-updated modules in `new Function(...)` calls, and the dev error overlay injects scripts that are not always nonce-tagged before hydration. This is documented in Next.js's own CSP guide, which explicitly recommends a conditional dev-vs-prod CSP for exactly this reason.\n\n## Concrete reproduction\n\n1. Developer pulls this branch (the very first sentence of `CLAUDE.md` under Commands says `npm run dev`).\n2. Runs `npm run dev`, opens `localhost:3000`.\n3. Page loads (SSR bootstrap scripts *are* nonce-tagged, so first paint works).\n4. Edits any component \u2192 save. React Refresh sends an HMR patch, dev runtime calls `new Function(moduleSource)` to install it.\n5. Browser blocks the eval: `Refused to evaluate a string as JavaScript because 'unsafe-eval' is not an allowed source of script in the following Content Security Policy directive: \"script-src 'self' 'nonce-...' 'strict-dynamic'\"`.\n6. HMR silently stops applying; developer sees no update or a broken reconnect loop. If a runtime error is thrown after that, the error overlay may also fail to inject.\n\n## Why the matcher does not exclude dev\n\nThe matcher only skips `/api`, `/_next/static`, `/_next/image`, `/favicon.ico`, and prefetches. Page navigations in dev receive the exact same CSP as production. React Refresh runs on those page responses.\n\n## Impact and fix\n\nProduction behavior is unchanged and correct. But `npm run dev` \u2014 the primary developer workflow, and the first command in the project's own onboarding docs \u2014 is degraded for every team member on first pull. This is a regression: `npm run dev` worked cleanly before this branch.\n\nTwo standard fixes; either is one line:\n\n```ts\n// Option A: skip strict CSP entirely in dev\nif (process.env.NODE_ENV !== \"production\") return NextResponse.next();\n\n// Option B: add unsafe-eval only in dev\n`script-src 'self' 'nonce-${nonce}' 'strict-dynamic'${process.env.NODE_ENV === \"production\" ? \"\" : \" 'unsafe-eval'\"}`\n```", "corroborated_by": null, "source_bug_ids": ["bug_002"], "created_at": 1786744413.1413927, "_file_path": "/tmp/bughunter_work/reports/bug_002_report.json"}, {"bug_id": "bug_001", "name": "Missing worker-src blocks pdfjs Worker under strict-dynamic CSP", "severity": "normal", "file_path": "proxy.ts", "start_line": 20, "end_line": 30, "pr_comment": "The new CSP declares `script-src 'self' 'nonce-...' 'strict-dynamic'` but no `worker-src`. Per CSP L3 \u00a76.1.10 the worker fetch falls through worker-src \u2192 child-src \u2192 script-src, and `'strict-dynamic'` invalidates `'self'` while Workers cannot carry a nonce, so pdfjs's worker (spawned from `app/lib/utils/fileExtraction.ts:26` and `app/lib/utils/pdfPropositionParser.ts:442`) is blocked. This silently breaks PDF upload (a documented supported input format) in spec-compliant browsers; fix by adding `worker-src 'self' blob:` to the directives array in `proxy.ts`.", "reasoning": "**What the bug is.** `proxy.ts` sets `script-src 'self' 'nonce-${nonce}' 'strict-dynamic'` and `default-src 'self'`, but declares no `worker-src` (and no `child-src`). Per W3C CSP Level 3 \u00a76.1.10, when a request for a worker script needs to be checked, the fallback chain is `worker-src \u2192 child-src \u2192 script-src \u2192 default-src`. Because neither `worker-src` nor `child-src` is present, the browser falls through to `script-src` \u2014 which contains `'strict-dynamic'`. Per CSP L3 \u00a76.6.2.1, `'strict-dynamic'` causes `'self'` and host-source expressions to be ignored; only nonce/hash-tagged sources (and their `\u003cscript\u003e`-tag descendants) pass. A `Worker` constructed via `new Worker(url)` cannot carry a script nonce and is not a `\u003cscript\u003e` descendant, so the load fails.\n\n**How it manifests.** `FileUpload.tsx` accepts `.pdf` uploads (documented in `CLAUDE.md` and `README.md`) and calls `extractTextFromPDF` in `app/lib/utils/fileExtraction.ts`. `getPdfjs()` at lines 24\u201330 lazily imports `pdfjs-dist` and sets:\n\n```ts\npdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = new URL(\n  \"pdfjs-dist/build/pdf.worker.min.mjs\",\n  import.meta.url,\n).toString();\n```\n\npdf.js internally does `new Worker(workerSrc)`. The same pattern exists in `app/lib/utils/pdfPropositionParser.ts:441-446`, which powers structured extraction for the decomposition pipeline. Both paths will emit a CSP violation and fail to instantiate the worker in Chrome/Firefox.\n\n**Step-by-step proof (Chrome, spec-compliant).**\n1. User navigates to `/`. `proxy.ts` generates a nonce, sets `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-abc' 'strict-dynamic'; \u2026`.\n2. User selects a `.pdf` file in `FileUpload`. Handler calls `extractTextFromPDF(file)`.\n3. `getPdfjs()` sets `GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc` to `https://localhost:3000/_next/static/media/pdf.worker.min.mjs` (or similar same-origin URL).\n4. `pdfjsLib.getDocument({data: buffer}).promise` triggers `new Worker(workerSrc)` under the hood.\n5. Browser CSP check for worker script fetch: no `worker-src`, no `child-src`, so uses `script-src`.\n6. `script-src` = `'self' 'nonce-abc' 'strict-dynamic'`. Per \u00a76.6.2.1, `'strict-dynamic'` present \u21d2 `'self'` ignored. Worker request has no nonce (workers cannot). Result: **blocked**.\n7. `new Worker(...)` throws / dispatches an error event. pdf.js promise rejects. Upload silently fails.\n\n**Why the existing CSP does not prevent it.** `default-src 'self'` would allow the worker, but it never gets consulted because the fallback stops at `script-src`. `'strict-dynamic'` is designed specifically to override permissive host allow-lists \u2014 that is what makes it strong for scripts and what breaks workers here.\n\n**Impact.** PDF ingestion is a first-class documented feature: `FileUpload` accepts `.txt, .doc, .docx, .pdf` and the decomposition pipeline invokes `extractStructuredItems`. Under this PR, PDF uploads will silently fail (only a CSP violation in the console) for every user in a spec-compliant browser. This is a direct regression introduced by this PR.\n\n**Fix.** Add one line to the `directives` array in `proxy.ts` at line 20\u201330:\n\n```ts\n\"worker-src 'self' blob:\",\n```\n\n`blob:` is included because some pdf.js code paths construct workers from a blob URL, and it is a well-established best practice for apps that spawn workers under nonce + `strict-dynamic` CSP (see Google's web.dev strict-CSP guide).", "corroborated_by": null, "source_bug_ids": ["bug_001"], "created_at": 1786744450.1779819, "_file_path": "/tmp/bughunter_work/reports/bug_001_report.json"}]</remote-review>